{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013y8PqtSVQ4zQxGRtCrF8E8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan — L1/092 GQA Attention with QK Norm (GLM-4.5-Air), A800 / sm_80\n2\t\n3\tExecutable, sequential KDA optimization plan. Derived from `docs/draft.md`, `task/definition.json`,\n4\t`task/feedback_workloads.jsonl`, `TASK.md`, `CLAUDE.md`. Triton-only compute; PyTorch for metadata /\n5\tlaunch plumbing only. This turn writes the plan only — **no candidate is implemented or evaluated.**\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 0. Ground rules (binding for every candidate)\n10\t\n11\t- **Entry point:** `solution/solution.py` exposes `run(hidden_states, q_proj_weight, q_proj_bias,\n12\t  k_proj_weight, k_proj_bias, v_proj_weight, v_proj_bias, o_proj_weight, q_norm_weight,\n13\t  k_norm_weight, cos, sin, rms_norm_eps)` and returns `output [B,S,4096]` bf16.\n14\t- **Compute = Triton only.** No `F.linear`, `torch.matmul`, `torch.bmm`, `scaled_dot_product_\n15\t  attention`, `torch.softmax`, `torch.rsqrt`-on-tensors-in-the-math-path, einsum, or CUDA-extension.\n16\t  PyTorch is allowed **only** for: `.shape`/stride reads, `torch.empty`/`empty_like` allocation,\n17\t  `.view`/`.reshape`/`.transpose` of metadata (no compute), dtype/device queries, grid-lambda\n18\t  construction, and passing pointers to kernels. Elementwise ops (RMSNorm, RoPE, bias, softmax,\n19\t  scaling) must live inside Triton kernels.\n20\t- **No fallback of any kind.** If a Triton kernel fails to compile/run, the candidate is invalid;\n21\t  fix the kernel — never route around it with Torch/NumPy/CPU.\n22\t- **Immutability & lineage.** Candidate source for `cNNN` is frozen once evaluated. Any change to\n23\t  source, autotune configs that alter the compiled artifact, launch grid, or dtype path ⇒ new ID.\n24\t  IDs are sequential (`c001, c002, …`), never reused.\n25\t- **Evaluation channel.** Only `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`. Five fixed workloads\n26\t  = one evaluation. Never run `final` without explicit operator approval. Never call the evaluator,\n27\t  a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, CUDA directly, or any alternate correctness harness.\n28\t- **Budgets.** ≤100 candidate evaluations; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M. Spend evals on hypotheses\n29\t  with a clear expected delta; do not burn evals on speculative micro-tweaks.\n30\t- **Skills.** `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200 profiling) are both\n31\t  out-of-scope for Ampere sm_80 and for the no-profiler rule; **not invoked**. Recorded as\n32\t  `skills_used: []` in every record with a one-line justification field.\n33\t\n34\t### 0.1 Fixed problem constants (hard-code in the kernels)\n35\t```\n36\tHIDDEN = 4096   NH = 96   NKV = 8   HEAD_DIM = 128   GROUPS = 12   HALF = 64\n37\tQ_OUT = 12288   KV_OUT = 1024   SCALE = 128 ** -0.5   # 0.08838834764831845\n38\t```\n39\t`B`, `S`, `M=B*S`, `rms_norm_eps` come from inputs at launch time.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## 1. Candidate lineage strategy\n44\t\n45\tTree-structured search. Each node = one immutable candidate; edges = single-hypothesis deltas from a\n46\tchosen parent (usually the current best-correct). **Correctness is a gate**: a candidate that fails\n47\tany workload cannot become a parent for perf work — only for a correctness-fix child.\n48\t\n49\t```\n50\tc001  correctness-first baseline (decomposition A, static tiles) ── GATE: must PASS all 5\n51\t  │\n52\t  ├─ c00x  GEMM tiling/autotune for projections + output GEMM (biggest FLOP share)\n53\t  │     │\n54\t  │     └─ c00x  flash-attention tile tuning + causal block early-exit\n55\t  │           │\n56\t  │           ├─ c00x  fuse RMSNorm+RoPE into attention prologue (option B)\n57\t  │           │\n58\t  │           └─ c00x  KV-load reuse across the 12-head group (memory-traffic cut)\n59\t  │\n60\t  └─ (correctness-fix branch only if c001 fails a workload)\n61\t```\n62\t\n63\tLineage rules:\n64\t1. **Advance the frontier** = best candidate that PASSes all five workloads, ranked by geomean.\n65\t2. Each new candidate changes **exactly one** design variable vs its parent (kernel fused/split,\n66\t   tiling, layout, precision path). This keeps attribution clean given feedback-only evidence.\n67\t3. If a change **regresses geomean or breaks correctness**, its parent remains the frontier; do not\n68\t   stack further changes on the regressor. Record the negative result and branch again from frontier.\n69\t4. Keep a small number of live hypotheses; prefer depth on the winning line over breadth.\n70\t\n71\t### 1.1 Concrete candidate ladder (initial; may adapt to feedback)\n72\t- **c001 — Baseline, correctness-first (decomposition A).**\n73\t  Kernels: (K1) Q proj GEMM +bias, (K2) K proj GEMM +bias, (K3) V proj GEMM +bias, (K4)\n74\t  RMSNorm+RoPE for Q, (K5) RMSNorm+RoPE for K, (K6) V repack into `[B,NKV,S,D]` (or read V in-place),\n75\t  (K7) flash-attention (GQA, causal, online softmax, fp32), (K8) output GEMM. Static, safe tile\n76\t  sizes (e.g. BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=64/128, BLOCK_K=32, num_warps=4, num_stages=2/3). No autotune yet\n77\t  (deterministic artifact, easy to reason about). **Goal: PASS ×5, establish baseline geomean.**\n78\t- **c002 — Projection + output GEMM performance.** Add `@triton.autotune` (or hand-picked shape-\n79\t  specialized configs) to K1–K3 and K8. Larger BLOCK_N for Q proj (N=12288) and output (N=4096,\n80\t  K=12288). Only-change: GEMM tiling. Hypothesis: recover cuBLAS-competitive GEMM throughput.\n81\t- **c003 — Flash-attention tiling + causal early-exit.** Tune BLOCK_M/BLOCK_N, num_warps/stages,\n82\t  skip fully-masked key blocks (`key_block_start > query_block_end`), tighten diagonal masking.\n83\t  Only-change: attention kernel.\n84\t- **c004 — Fuse RMSNorm+RoPE into attention prologue (option B).** Attention loads raw projected\n85\t  Q/K tiles, applies norm+RoPE on the fly; drop K4/K5 and their Q/K round-trip. Only-change:\n86\t  norm/RoPE fusion. (Guard: K-tile recompute cost vs saved memory traffic.)\n87\t- **c005 — KV-load reuse across the group of 12.** One program services a block of query heads that\n88\t  share a KV head, so K/V tiles are loaded once per group. Only-change: attention grid/tiling.\n89\t- **c006+ — conditional:** K/V fused projection (option C) if K/V proj shows up as non-trivial;\n90\t  split-K for the M=128 workload if it under-utilizes SMs; precision-margin variants if any\n91\t  workload is correct-but-marginal.\n92\t\n93\tExact IDs beyond c001–c005 depend on measured deltas; the ladder is re-planned after each eval.\n94\t\n95\t---\n96\t\n97\t## 2. Correctness checks (static, pre-evaluation, per candidate)\n98\t\n99\tBecause local execution is prohibited, every candidate passes a **static review checklist** before\n100\tconsuming an evaluation. Reject/rewrite before eval if any item is uncertain.\n101\t\n102\t### 2.1 Math-equivalence checklist (against draft §1.2)\n103\t- [ ] **Projections** compute `x @ W^T + bias` (W stored `[N,K]`; reduce over K=4096). Bias added in\n104\t      fp32, result cast bf16. Q→`[M,12288]`, K/V→`[M,1024]`.\n105\t- [ ] **Head reshape** consistent: Q `[B,S,96,128]`, K/V `[B,S,8,128]`; strides correct.\n106\t- [ ] **RMSNorm** over last dim 128, in **fp32**: `mean(x²)`, `rsqrt(var+eps)`, `× weight`, cast bf16.\n107\t      Uses `q_norm_weight` for Q, `k_norm_weight` for K. `eps` is the fp32 scalar `rms_norm_eps`.\n108\t- [ ] **RoPE** applied after norm, elementwise over full 128 width:\n109\t      `out = x*cos + rotate_half(x)*sin`, `rotate_half(x) = cat(-x[64:128], x[0:64])`.\n110\t      Sign: low half (0..63) gets `-x[64:128]·sin`; high half (64..127) gets `+x[0:64]·sin`.\n111\t      `cos/sin` indexed at `[b, s, 0:128]`, broadcast across heads. **Do not assume half-duplication.**\n112\t- [ ] **GQA mapping** `kv_head = q_head // 12` (integer). No physical 12× KV expansion.\n113\t- [ ] **Scores** `= (q·k) * SCALE`, reduce over 128 in fp32.\n114\t- [ ] **Causal mask** keeps `key_j ≤ query_i` (diagonal included), masks `j > i` with `-inf`.\n115\t      No fully-masked row (diagonal always present) ⇒ no NaN.\n116\t- [ ] **Softmax** in fp32 (online max/sum); accumulate `p·v` in fp32; cast attn output bf16.\n117\t- [ ] **Head merge** → `[B,S,12288]` row-major matching reference `transpose(1,2).reshape`.\n118\t- [ ] **Output proj** `attn @ o_proj_weight^T`, reduce over K=12288 in fp32, **no bias**, →`[B,S,4096]`.\n119\t- [ ] **Boundary masking** for non-pow2 S (293, 373) and small M (128): OOB rows load 0 / store\n120\t      masked; OOB keys get `-inf` score; OOB reductions contribute 0.\n121\t- [ ] **Dtypes:** all DRAM tensors bf16; all accumulators/reductions fp32; output bf16.\n122\t\n123\t### 2.2 Structural checks\n124\t- [ ] No Torch compute op in `run` (grep the source for `F.linear`, `matmul`, `bmm`, `softmax`,\n125\t      `scaled_dot_product_attention`, `einsum`, `@` on tensors) before evaluating.\n126\t- [ ] Grid dimensions cover all (B, heads, M-blocks, N-blocks); no partial coverage.\n127\t- [ ] Strides passed explicitly; no accidental reliance on contiguity that the harness may not give.\n128\t- [ ] Output tensor allocated once, correct shape/dtype/device; returned directly.\n129\t\n130\t### 2.3 Correctness evidence from evaluation\n131\t- The feedback evaluator reports per-workload pass/fail against `(atol, rtol)` from\n132\t  `feedback_workloads.jsonl`. Treat **any** failing workload as a correctness bug to localize:\n133\t  - Only non-pow2 S fail → boundary masking bug.\n134\t  - Only M=128 fail → grid/coverage or degenerate-tile bug.\n135\t  - All fail with small margin → a systematic precision path issue (accumulation/rounding).\n136\t  - All fail large → structural bug (GQA map, RoPE sign, transpose/stride, causal direction).\n137\t\n138\t---\n139\t\n140\t## 3. Performance hypotheses (each maps to a candidate)\n141\t\n142\t| # | Hypothesis | Change | Expected effect | Risk |\n143\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n144\t| H1 | A correct flash-style attention (no S² materialization, no 12× KV expand) beats the reference's materialized path. | c001 attention kernel. | Attention memory traffic ↓ ~10× vs reference; net geomean > 1 even with untuned GEMMs. | GEMMs may be slow enough to offset; measured in c001. |\n145\t| H2 | Projection + output GEMMs are the FLOP bottleneck; tiling/autotune brings them near cuBLAS. | c002 GEMM configs. | Largest absolute time reduction (GEMMs ≈ 650 GFLOP vs 55 attention). | Triton GEMM may still trail cuBLAS on some shapes ⇒ net-neutral. |\n146\t| H3 | Attention tile tuning + causal block-skip halves attention work (triangular). | c003. | ~2× on attention portion for large S (1024, 512). | Small for tiny S (128). |\n147\t| H4 | Fusing RMSNorm+RoPE into attention removes a Q/K round-trip. | c004. | Q is `[M,12288]` bf16 = large; removing its write+read is a real DRAM saving. | K-tile norm/RoPE recompute across M-blocks; net depends on S. |\n148\t| H5 | KV-load reuse across the 12-head group cuts K/V DRAM reads ~12×. | c005. | Meaningful for larger B·heads; improves occupancy-bound cases. | Register pressure / occupancy drop. |\n149\t| H6 | M=128 workload under-utilizes SMs; split-K or smaller M-tiles help. | c006 (conditional). | Better small-M throughput. | Split-K adds a reduction pass. |\n150\t\n151\tPerformance evidence = **geomean speedup** across the five workloads reported by the evaluator, plus\n152\tper-workload speedups (to see which shape a change helps/hurts). A change is **kept** only if it\n153\timproves geomean without breaking any workload's correctness; otherwise reverted (branch stays at\n154\tparent).\n155\t\n156\t---\n157\t\n158\t## 4. Stopping criteria\n159\t\n160\tStop the search and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` (with the reason) when **any** holds:\n161\t1. **Convergence:** geomean improvement < ~1–2% across ≥3 consecutive candidates on the frontier\n162\t   line, and no untried hypothesis has a plausible >5% expected delta.\n163\t2. **Budget:** approaching 100 evaluations or the 1.0M-token soft limit (leave margin below the 1.2M\n164\t   hard limit; stop implementing new candidates once soft limit is near).\n165\t3. **Ceiling reached:** GEMMs are cuBLAS-competitive and attention is flash-optimal with KV reuse —\n166\t   remaining ops are memory-bound at hardware roofline; no structural win left.\n167\t\n168\tAt stop: the frontier (best PASS-all-five candidate by geomean) is the recommended candidate for the\n169\toperator-approved `final` run. **Do not run `final` autonomously.**\n170\t\n171\tRoll-back / branch policy during search:\n172\t- Regressor (worse geomean, still correct): discard branch; note in record; branch from frontier.\n173\t- Correctness break: mandatory fix-child before any perf work continues on that line.\n174\t- Marginal-correct workload (passes but near tolerance): prefer the more reference-faithful precision\n175\t  variant (bf16-intermediate RoPE; match reference softmax bf16-rounding of `p`) even at small cost.\n176\t\n177\t---\n178\t\n179\t## 5. Evidence format (append-only `candidates.jsonl`)\n180\t\n181\tOne JSON object appended per evaluated candidate. Never rewrite earlier records. Schema:\n182\t\n183\t```json\n184\t{\n185\t  \"candidate\": \"c001\",\n186\t  \"parent\": null,\n187\t  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution/solution.py at eval time>\",\n188\t  \"timestamp\": \"<ISO-8601>\",\n189\t  \"hypothesis\": \"Correct flash-style GQA attention + Triton GEMMs beats materialized reference.\",\n190\t  \"design_delta\": \"baseline: decomposition A, static tiles, no autotune\",\n191\t  \"static_validation\": {\n192\t    \"math_checklist\": \"pass\",\n193\t    \"structural_checklist\": \"pass\",\n194\t    \"notes\": \"GQA kv_head=h//12; causal j<=i; RoPE full-128 rotate_half; fp32 accum\"\n195\t  },\n196\t  \"per_workload\": [\n197\t    {\"uuid\": \"ccea3f00\", \"B\": 1, \"S\": 128,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n198\t    {\"uuid\": \"1e96cc88\", \"B\": 4, \"S\": 293,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n199\t    {\"uuid\": \"d0ce8c45\", \"B\": 1, \"S\": 1024, \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n200\t    {\"uuid\": \"c92bb0a5\", \"B\": 1, \"S\": 512,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n201\t    {\"uuid\": \"65a1864d\", \"B\": 8, \"S\": 373,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00}\n202\t  ],\n203\t  \"all_correct\": true,\n204\t  \"geomean_speedup\": 0.00,\n205\t  \"decision\": \"keep|revert|fix\",\n206\t  \"reason\": \"<why kept/reverted; what the per-workload pattern shows>\",\n207\t  \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1,\n208\t  \"skills_used\": [],\n209\t  \"skills_note\": \"KernelWiki/ncu-report-skill out-of-scope for sm_80 + no-profiler rule\",\n210\t  \"next\": \"c002: autotune projection + output GEMMs\"\n211\t}\n212\t```\n213\t\n214\tRules:\n215\t- `speedup` and `geomean_speedup` are copied from the evaluator output verbatim (no re-derivation of\n216\t  correctness; the evaluator is the sole correctness/perf authority).\n217\t- `cumulative_evaluations` increments by 1 per candidate (5 workloads = 1 eval).\n218\t- If a workload fails, set `correct: false`, `all_correct: false`, `decision: \"fix\"`, and record the\n219\t  localized bug class in `reason`.\n220\t- `source_sha256` recorded so immutability is auditable (compute with `sha256sum solution/solution.py`\n221\t  at eval time; do not edit a candidate's source after its record is written).\n222\t\n223\t---\n224\t\n225\t## 6. Execution checklist (per candidate, in order)\n226\t\n227\t1. Choose parent = current frontier (or fix-target). State the single design delta.\n228\t2. Implement `solution/solution.py` for the new candidate ID (write the Triton kernels + `run`).\n229\t3. Run the static correctness checklist (§2.1/§2.2). Fix until all items are confident.\n230\t4. Snapshot immutability: record `sha256sum solution/solution.py`.\n231\t5. Evaluate once: `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`.\n232\t6. Append the evidence record (§5) to `candidates.jsonl`.\n233\t7. Decide keep/revert/fix; update the frontier; plan the next single delta.\n234\t8. Check stopping criteria (§4). If converged → write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with reason. Else loop.\n235\t\n236\t---\n237\t\n238\t## 7. Immediate next action (next turn, not this one)\n239\tImplement **c001** (decomposition A, correctness-first) exactly per §1.1 and §2.1, then evaluate once.\n240\tNo `plan.md`/`draft.md` edits required to proceed.\n241\t\n242\t---\n243\t\n244\t## 8. Decision log (updated after each eval)\n245\t\n246\t### c001 — evaluated (eval #1). DECISION: keep. **Frontier = c001.**\n247\t- Result: **PASS 5/5, geomean 1.7135x**, avg 1.7324x (source_sha256 `81c9240…`).\n248\t- Per-workload speedup: S=128 →1.4649, S=293(B4) →1.7034, S=1024 →2.2476, S=512 →1.6510,\n249\t  S=373(B8) →1.5951.\n250\t- Reading: H1 confirmed — flash-style attention already beats the materialized reference across the\n251\t  board with no correctness issue (loose `rtol=0.05` tolerated fp32 online softmax fine). The\n252\t  smallest gains are the compute-heavy cases (tiny M=128 launch overhead; largest M=2984 where the\n253\t  untuned 64×64×32 GEMMs cap throughput), which points at **H2 (GEMM tiling)** as the next lever.\n254\t- Next: **c002** — shape-specialized tiling/autotune for the projection + output GEMMs (biggest FLOP\n255\t  share), single design delta vs c001, everything else identical.\n256\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8df798b-a9b9-45b8-9fc1-9cba86dca820","uuid":"f770854e-f3ed-4191-b88e-3ce6c171b7d9","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:52:44.990Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-092_gqa_attention_with_qk_norm/docs/plan.md","content":"# Plan — L1/092 GQA Attention with QK Norm (GLM-4.5-Air), A800 / sm_80\n\nExecutable, sequential KDA optimization plan. Derived from `docs/draft.md`, `task/definition.json`,\n`task/feedback_workloads.jsonl`, `TASK.md`, `CLAUDE.md`. Triton-only compute; PyTorch for metadata /\nlaunch plumbing only. This turn writes the plan only — **no candidate is implemented or evaluated.**\n\n---\n\n## 0. Ground rules (binding for every candidate)\n\n- **Entry point:** `solution/solution.py` exposes `run(hidden_states, q_proj_weight, q_proj_bias,\n  k_proj_weight, k_proj_bias, v_proj_weight, v_proj_bias, o_proj_weight, q_norm_weight,\n  k_norm_weight, cos, sin, rms_norm_eps)` and returns `output [B,S,4096]` bf16.\n- **Compute = Triton only.** No `F.linear`, `torch.matmul`, `torch.bmm`, `scaled_dot_product_\n  attention`, `torch.softmax`, `torch.rsqrt`-on-tensors-in-the-math-path, einsum, or CUDA-extension.\n  PyTorch is allowed **only** for: `.shape`/stride reads, `torch.empty`/`empty_like` allocation,\n  `.view`/`.reshape`/`.transpose` of metadata (no compute), dtype/device queries, grid-lambda\n  construction, and passing pointers to kernels. Elementwise ops (RMSNorm, RoPE, bias, softmax,\n  scaling) must live inside Triton kernels.\n- **No fallback of any kind.** If a Triton kernel fails to compile/run, the candidate is invalid;\n  fix the kernel — never route around it with Torch/NumPy/CPU.\n- **Immutability & lineage.** Candidate source for `cNNN` is frozen once evaluated. Any change to\n  source, autotune configs that alter the compiled artifact, launch grid, or dtype path ⇒ new ID.\n  IDs are sequential (`c001, c002, …`), never reused.\n- **Evaluation channel.** Only `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`. Five fixed workloads\n  = one evaluation. Never run `final` without explicit operator approval. Never call the evaluator,\n  a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, CUDA directly, or any alternate correctness harness.\n- **Budgets.** ≤100 candidate evaluations; token soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M. Spend evals on hypotheses\n  with a clear expected delta; do not burn evals on speculative micro-tweaks.\n- **Skills.** `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200 profiling) are both\n  out-of-scope for Ampere sm_80 and for the no-profiler rule; **not invoked**. Recorded as\n  `skills_used: []` in every record with a one-line justification field.\n\n### 0.1 Fixed problem constants (hard-code in the kernels)\n```\nHIDDEN = 4096   NH = 96   NKV = 8   HEAD_DIM = 128   GROUPS = 12   HALF = 64\nQ_OUT = 12288   KV_OUT = 1024   SCALE = 128 ** -0.5   # 0.08838834764831845\n```\n`B`, `S`, `M=B*S`, `rms_norm_eps` come from inputs at launch time.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Candidate lineage strategy\n\nTree-structured search. Each node = one immutable candidate; edges = single-hypothesis deltas from a\nchosen parent (usually the current best-correct). **Correctness is a gate**: a candidate that fails\nany workload cannot become a parent for perf work — only for a correctness-fix child.\n\n```\nc001  correctness-first baseline (decomposition A, static tiles) ── GATE: must PASS all 5\n  │\n  ├─ c00x  GEMM tiling/autotune for projections + output GEMM (biggest FLOP share)\n  │     │\n  │     └─ c00x  flash-attention tile tuning + causal block early-exit\n  │           │\n  │           ├─ c00x  fuse RMSNorm+RoPE into attention prologue (option B)\n  │           │\n  │           └─ c00x  KV-load reuse across the 12-head group (memory-traffic cut)\n  │\n  └─ (correctness-fix branch only if c001 fails a workload)\n```\n\nLineage rules:\n1. **Advance the frontier** = best candidate that PASSes all five workloads, ranked by geomean.\n2. Each new candidate changes **exactly one** design variable vs its parent (kernel fused/split,\n   tiling, layout, precision path). This keeps attribution clean given feedback-only evidence.\n3. If a change **regresses geomean or breaks correctness**, its parent remains the frontier; do not\n   stack further changes on the regressor. Record the negative result and branch again from frontier.\n4. Keep a small number of live hypotheses; prefer depth on the winning line over breadth.\n\n### 1.1 Concrete candidate ladder (initial; may adapt to feedback)\n- **c001 — Baseline, correctness-first (decomposition A).**\n  Kernels: (K1) Q proj GEMM +bias, (K2) K proj GEMM +bias, (K3) V proj GEMM +bias, (K4)\n  RMSNorm+RoPE for Q, (K5) RMSNorm+RoPE for K, (K6) V repack into `[B,NKV,S,D]` (or read V in-place),\n  (K7) flash-attention (GQA, causal, online softmax, fp32), (K8) output GEMM. Static, safe tile\n  sizes (e.g. BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=64/128, BLOCK_K=32, num_warps=4, num_stages=2/3). No autotune yet\n  (deterministic artifact, easy to reason about). **Goal: PASS ×5, establish baseline geomean.**\n- **c002 — Projection + output GEMM performance.** Add `@triton.autotune` (or hand-picked shape-\n  specialized configs) to K1–K3 and K8. Larger BLOCK_N for Q proj (N=12288) and output (N=4096,\n  K=12288). Only-change: GEMM tiling. Hypothesis: recover cuBLAS-competitive GEMM throughput.\n- **c003 — Flash-attention tiling + causal early-exit.** Tune BLOCK_M/BLOCK_N, num_warps/stages,\n  skip fully-masked key blocks (`key_block_start > query_block_end`), tighten diagonal masking.\n  Only-change: attention kernel.\n- **c004 — Fuse RMSNorm+RoPE into attention prologue (option B).** Attention loads raw projected\n  Q/K tiles, applies norm+RoPE on the fly; drop K4/K5 and their Q/K round-trip. Only-change:\n  norm/RoPE fusion. (Guard: K-tile recompute cost vs saved memory traffic.)\n- **c005 — KV-load reuse across the group of 12.** One program services a block of query heads that\n  share a KV head, so K/V tiles are loaded once per group. Only-change: attention grid/tiling.\n- **c006+ — conditional:** K/V fused projection (option C) if K/V proj shows up as non-trivial;\n  split-K for the M=128 workload if it under-utilizes SMs; precision-margin variants if any\n  workload is correct-but-marginal.\n\nExact IDs beyond c001–c005 depend on measured deltas; the ladder is re-planned after each eval.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Correctness checks (static, pre-evaluation, per candidate)\n\nBecause local execution is prohibited, every candidate passes a **static review checklist** before\nconsuming an evaluation. Reject/rewrite before eval if any item is uncertain.\n\n### 2.1 Math-equivalence checklist (against draft §1.2)\n- [ ] **Projections** compute `x @ W^T + bias` (W stored `[N,K]`; reduce over K=4096). Bias added in\n      fp32, result cast bf16. Q→`[M,12288]`, K/V→`[M,1024]`.\n- [ ] **Head reshape** consistent: Q `[B,S,96,128]`, K/V `[B,S,8,128]`; strides correct.\n- [ ] **RMSNorm** over last dim 128, in **fp32**: `mean(x²)`, `rsqrt(var+eps)`, `× weight`, cast bf16.\n      Uses `q_norm_weight` for Q, `k_norm_weight` for K. `eps` is the fp32 scalar `rms_norm_eps`.\n- [ ] **RoPE** applied after norm, elementwise over full 128 width:\n      `out = x*cos + rotate_half(x)*sin`, `rotate_half(x) = cat(-x[64:128], x[0:64])`.\n      Sign: low half (0..63) gets `-x[64:128]·sin`; high half (64..127) gets `+x[0:64]·sin`.\n      `cos/sin` indexed at `[b, s, 0:128]`, broadcast across heads. **Do not assume half-duplication.**\n- [ ] **GQA mapping** `kv_head = q_head // 12` (integer). No physical 12× KV expansion.\n- [ ] **Scores** `= (q·k) * SCALE`, reduce over 128 in fp32.\n- [ ] **Causal mask** keeps `key_j ≤ query_i` (diagonal included), masks `j > i` with `-inf`.\n      No fully-masked row (diagonal always present) ⇒ no NaN.\n- [ ] **Softmax** in fp32 (online max/sum); accumulate `p·v` in fp32; cast attn output bf16.\n- [ ] **Head merge** → `[B,S,12288]` row-major matching reference `transpose(1,2).reshape`.\n- [ ] **Output proj** `attn @ o_proj_weight^T`, reduce over K=12288 in fp32, **no bias**, →`[B,S,4096]`.\n- [ ] **Boundary masking** for non-pow2 S (293, 373) and small M (128): OOB rows load 0 / store\n      masked; OOB keys get `-inf` score; OOB reductions contribute 0.\n- [ ] **Dtypes:** all DRAM tensors bf16; all accumulators/reductions fp32; output bf16.\n\n### 2.2 Structural checks\n- [ ] No Torch compute op in `run` (grep the source for `F.linear`, `matmul`, `bmm`, `softmax`,\n      `scaled_dot_product_attention`, `einsum`, `@` on tensors) before evaluating.\n- [ ] Grid dimensions cover all (B, heads, M-blocks, N-blocks); no partial coverage.\n- [ ] Strides passed explicitly; no accidental reliance on contiguity that the harness may not give.\n- [ ] Output tensor allocated once, correct shape/dtype/device; returned directly.\n\n### 2.3 Correctness evidence from evaluation\n- The feedback evaluator reports per-workload pass/fail against `(atol, rtol)` from\n  `feedback_workloads.jsonl`. Treat **any** failing workload as a correctness bug to localize:\n  - Only non-pow2 S fail → boundary masking bug.\n  - Only M=128 fail → grid/coverage or degenerate-tile bug.\n  - All fail with small margin → a systematic precision path issue (accumulation/rounding).\n  - All fail large → structural bug (GQA map, RoPE sign, transpose/stride, causal direction).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Performance hypotheses (each maps to a candidate)\n\n| # | Hypothesis | Change | Expected effect | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 | A correct flash-style attention (no S² materialization, no 12× KV expand) beats the reference's materialized path. | c001 attention kernel. | Attention memory traffic ↓ ~10× vs reference; net geomean > 1 even with untuned GEMMs. | GEMMs may be slow enough to offset; measured in c001. |\n| H2 | Projection + output GEMMs are the FLOP bottleneck; tiling/autotune brings them near cuBLAS. | c002 GEMM configs. | Largest absolute time reduction (GEMMs ≈ 650 GFLOP vs 55 attention). | Triton GEMM may still trail cuBLAS on some shapes ⇒ net-neutral. |\n| H3 | Attention tile tuning + causal block-skip halves attention work (triangular). | c003. | ~2× on attention portion for large S (1024, 512). | Small for tiny S (128). |\n| H4 | Fusing RMSNorm+RoPE into attention removes a Q/K round-trip. | c004. | Q is `[M,12288]` bf16 = large; removing its write+read is a real DRAM saving. | K-tile norm/RoPE recompute across M-blocks; net depends on S. |\n| H5 | KV-load reuse across the 12-head group cuts K/V DRAM reads ~12×. | c005. | Meaningful for larger B·heads; improves occupancy-bound cases. | Register pressure / occupancy drop. |\n| H6 | M=128 workload under-utilizes SMs; split-K or smaller M-tiles help. | c006 (conditional). | Better small-M throughput. | Split-K adds a reduction pass. |\n\nPerformance evidence = **geomean speedup** across the five workloads reported by the evaluator, plus\nper-workload speedups (to see which shape a change helps/hurts). A change is **kept** only if it\nimproves geomean without breaking any workload's correctness; otherwise reverted (branch stays at\nparent).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Stopping criteria\n\nStop the search and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` (with the reason) when **any** holds:\n1. **Convergence:** geomean improvement < ~1–2% across ≥3 consecutive candidates on the frontier\n   line, and no untried hypothesis has a plausible >5% expected delta.\n2. **Budget:** approaching 100 evaluations or the 1.0M-token soft limit (leave margin below the 1.2M\n   hard limit; stop implementing new candidates once soft limit is near).\n3. **Ceiling reached:** GEMMs are cuBLAS-competitive and attention is flash-optimal with KV reuse —\n   remaining ops are memory-bound at hardware roofline; no structural win left.\n\nAt stop: the frontier (best PASS-all-five candidate by geomean) is the recommended candidate for the\noperator-approved `final` run. **Do not run `final` autonomously.**\n\nRoll-back / branch policy during search:\n- Regressor (worse geomean, still correct): discard branch; note in record; branch from frontier.\n- Correctness break: mandatory fix-child before any perf work continues on that line.\n- Marginal-correct workload (passes but near tolerance): prefer the more reference-faithful precision\n  variant (bf16-intermediate RoPE; match reference softmax bf16-rounding of `p`) even at small cost.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Evidence format (append-only `candidates.jsonl`)\n\nOne JSON object appended per evaluated candidate. Never rewrite earlier records. Schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"candidate\": \"c001\",\n  \"parent\": null,\n  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution/solution.py at eval time>\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"<ISO-8601>\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Correct flash-style GQA attention + Triton GEMMs beats materialized reference.\",\n  \"design_delta\": \"baseline: decomposition A, static tiles, no autotune\",\n  \"static_validation\": {\n    \"math_checklist\": \"pass\",\n    \"structural_checklist\": \"pass\",\n    \"notes\": \"GQA kv_head=h//12; causal j<=i; RoPE full-128 rotate_half; fp32 accum\"\n  },\n  \"per_workload\": [\n    {\"uuid\": \"ccea3f00\", \"B\": 1, \"S\": 128,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n    {\"uuid\": \"1e96cc88\", \"B\": 4, \"S\": 293,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n    {\"uuid\": \"d0ce8c45\", \"B\": 1, \"S\": 1024, \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n    {\"uuid\": \"c92bb0a5\", \"B\": 1, \"S\": 512,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00},\n    {\"uuid\": \"65a1864d\", \"B\": 8, \"S\": 373,  \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 0.00}\n  ],\n  \"all_correct\": true,\n  \"geomean_speedup\": 0.00,\n  \"decision\": \"keep|revert|fix\",\n  \"reason\": \"<why kept/reverted; what the per-workload pattern shows>\",\n  \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1,\n  \"skills_used\": [],\n  \"skills_note\": \"KernelWiki/ncu-report-skill out-of-scope for sm_80 + no-profiler rule\",\n  \"next\": \"c002: autotune projection + output GEMMs\"\n}\n```\n\nRules:\n- `speedup` and `geomean_speedup` are copied from the evaluator output verbatim (no re-derivation of\n  correctness; the evaluator is the sole correctness/perf authority).\n- `cumulative_evaluations` increments by 1 per candidate (5 workloads = 1 eval).\n- If a workload fails, set `correct: false`, `all_correct: false`, `decision: \"fix\"`, and record the\n  localized bug class in `reason`.\n- `source_sha256` recorded so immutability is auditable (compute with `sha256sum solution/solution.py`\n  at eval time; do not edit a candidate's source after its record is written).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Execution checklist (per candidate, in order)\n\n1. Choose parent = current frontier (or fix-target). State the single design delta.\n2. Implement `solution/solution.py` for the new candidate ID (write the Triton kernels + `run`).\n3. Run the static correctness checklist (§2.1/§2.2). Fix until all items are confident.\n4. Snapshot immutability: record `sha256sum solution/solution.py`.\n5. Evaluate once: `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`.\n6. Append the evidence record (§5) to `candidates.jsonl`.\n7. Decide keep/revert/fix; update the frontier; plan the next single delta.\n8. Check stopping criteria (§4). If converged → write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with reason. Else loop.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Immediate next action (next turn, not this one)\nImplement **c001** (decomposition A, correctness-first) exactly per §1.1 and §2.1, then evaluate once.\nNo `plan.md`/`draft.md` edits required to proceed.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Decision log (updated after each eval)\n\n### c001 — evaluated (eval #1). DECISION: keep. **Frontier = c001.**\n- Result: **PASS 5/5, geomean 1.7135x**, avg 1.7324x (source_sha256 `81c9240…`).\n- Per-workload speedup: S=128 →1.4649, S=293(B4) →1.7034, S=1024 →2.2476, S=512 →1.6510,\n  S=373(B8) →1.5951.\n- Reading: H1 confirmed — flash-style attention already beats the materialized reference across the\n  board with no correctness issue (loose `rtol=0.05` tolerated fp32 online softmax fine). The\n  smallest gains are the compute-heavy cases (tiny M=128 launch overhead; largest M=2984 where the\n  untuned 64×64×32 GEMMs cap throughput), which points at **H2 (GEMM tiling)** as the next lever.\n- Next: **c002** — shape-specialized tiling/autotune for the projection + output GEMMs (biggest FLOP\n  share), single design delta vs c001, everything else identical.\n","numLines":256,"startLine":1,"totalLines":256}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KXDtCPSW68Xds3WxEw1J9T","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L1/092 GQA Attention with QK Norm (GLM-4.5-Air)\n2\t\n3\tTarget GPU: **NVIDIA A800, `sm_80` (Ampere)**. Primary implementation must be **Triton**;\n4\tPyTorch permitted only for tensor metadata / launch plumbing (no Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension\n5\tcompute fallback). Entry point: `solution/solution.py:run(...)` with the exact signature from\n6\t`task/definition.json`.\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 1. Operation analysis\n11\t\n12\t### 1.1 Fixed configuration (from `definition.json`)\n13\t- `hidden_size = 4096`\n14\t- `num_attention_heads = 96`, `head_dim = 128` → `q_out_features = 96*128 = 12288`\n15\t- `num_key_value_heads = 8` → `kv_out_features = 8*128 = 1024`\n16\t- `num_key_value_groups = 96 / 8 = 12` (each KV head is shared by 12 query heads)\n17\t- `head_dim/2 = 64` (RoPE rotate-half split point)\n18\t- `scaling = head_dim**-0.5 = 128**-0.5 ≈ 0.0883883476`\n19\t- Variable axes: `batch_size (B)`, `seq_len (S)`. All tensors `bfloat16` except `rms_norm_eps` (fp32 scalar).\n20\t\n21\t### 1.2 Reference dataflow (must be numerically reproduced)\n22\tGiven `hidden_states [B,S,4096]`:\n23\t\n24\t1. **Projections** (`F.linear`, i.e. `x @ W^T + b`):\n25\t   - `q = hidden @ q_proj_weight^T + q_proj_bias` → `[B,S,12288]`\n26\t   - `k = hidden @ k_proj_weight^T + k_proj_bias` → `[B,S,1024]`\n27\t   - `v = hidden @ v_proj_weight^T + v_proj_bias` → `[B,S,1024]`\n28\t2. **Reshape to heads**: q→`[B,S,96,128]`, k,v→`[B,S,8,128]`.\n29\t3. **QK RMSNorm** over the last axis (`head_dim=128`), computed in **fp32**:\n30\t   `x_f32 = x.float(); var = mean(x_f32^2, -1, keepdim); x = x_f32 * rsqrt(var + eps);\n31\t    out = (weight * x).to(bf16)`. Applied to q (with `q_norm_weight`) and k (with `k_norm_weight`).\n32\t   **Result is cast back to bf16 *before* RoPE.**\n33\t4. **Transpose** to `[B, H, S, D]` (q: H=96, k/v: H=8).\n34\t5. **RoPE** (`cos`,`sin` are `[B,S,128]`, `unsqueeze(1)`→`[B,1,S,128]`):\n35\t   `rotate_half(x) = cat(-x[...,64:], x[...,:64])`;\n36\t   `x = x*cos + rotate_half(x)*sin`. Applied to q and k in **bf16** (inputs are already bf16).\n37\t   NOTE: `cos`/`sin` are arbitrary random bf16 tensors of full width 128 in the test harness — they are\n38\t   **not** guaranteed to be true cos/sin nor half-duplicated; apply the formula literally element-wise.\n39\t6. **GQA expand**: k,v repeated 8→96 heads (12× each).\n40\t7. **Attention**: `scores = (q @ k^T) * scaling` → `[B,96,S,S]`;\n41\t   add causal mask (`triu(diagonal=1) = -inf`, i.e. key index `> query index` masked);\n42\t   `p = softmax(scores, dim=-1, dtype=fp32).to(bf16)`; `attn = p @ v` → `[B,96,S,128]`.\n43\t8. **Merge heads**: transpose→`[B,S,96,128]`→reshape `[B,S,12288]`.\n44\t9. **Output projection** (no bias): `out = attn @ o_proj_weight^T` → `[B,S,4096]`.\n45\t\n46\t### 1.3 Feedback workloads (five fixed; one eval covers all five)\n47\t| uuid short | B | S | M=B·S | atol | rtol |\n48\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n49\t| ccea3f00 | 1 | 128 | 128 | 0.0034 | 0.05 |\n50\t| 1e96cc88 | 4 | 293 | 1172 | 0.0037 | 0.05 |\n51\t| d0ce8c45 | 1 | 1024 | 1024 | 0.0030 | 0.05 |\n52\t| c92bb0a5 | 1 | 512 | 512 | 0.0025 | 0.05 |\n53\t| 65a1864d | 8 | 373 | 2984 | 0.0038 | 0.05 |\n54\t\n55\tObservations:\n56\t- `S ∈ {128, 293, 373, 512, 1024}` — **two are non-power-of-two (293, 373)**, so every kernel\n57\t  that tiles over `S` (projection M-dim, attention seq-dim) must mask boundary tiles.\n58\t- `M = B·S ∈ {128 … 2984}` — GEMM M-dim is modest; the projection GEMMs are \"tall-skinny→wide\"\n59\t  (`N`=12288 for Q, `K`=4096) and the output GEMM is `N`=4096, `K`=12288.\n60\t- Small S means the `O(S²)` attention is cheap **in FLOPs** but the reference pays a large\n61\t  **memory** cost (materialized `[B,96,S,S]` scores + fp32 softmax buffer + 12× KV expansion).\n62\t\n63\t### 1.4 Roofline / where time goes (rough FLOP accounting, per workload, ×2 for MAC)\n64\tFor the largest case B=8,S=373 (M=2984):\n65\t- Q proj: `M·12288·4096·2 ≈ 300 GFLOP`; K proj + V proj: `M·1024·4096·2·2 ≈ 50 GFLOP`.\n66\t- Attention (QK^T + PV): `B·96·S²·128·2·2 ≈ 8·96·373²·128·4 ≈ 55 GFLOP`.\n67\t- Output proj: `M·4096·12288·2 ≈ 300 GFLOP`.\n68\t\n69\t⇒ **GEMMs dominate FLOPs** (~650 GFLOP vs ~55 GFLOP attention). The reference runs GEMMs on\n70\tcuBLAS (hard to beat) but runs attention as a **naive materialized path with a 12× KV blow-up**.\n71\tTherefore the most reliable speedup source is **fusing/streaming attention (flash-style, no S²\n72\tmaterialization, no KV expansion)**, while the projection/output GEMMs must be re-implemented in\n73\tTriton *at least competitively* with cuBLAS so they don't erase the attention win.\n74\t\n75\t---\n76\t\n77\t## 2. Constraints (from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n78\t\n79\t- **Triton-only compute.** No `torch.matmul`/`F.linear`/SDPA in the compute path — those count as a\n80\t  Torch computational fallback → invalid. The three projections, attention, and output projection\n81\t  must all be Triton kernels. PyTorch may only allocate/reshape/stride and launch.\n82\t- **A failed Triton kernel is invalid** — no silent Torch fallback allowed.\n83\t- **Immutability**: candidates `c001, c002, …` are append-only; new source ⇒ new ID.\n84\t- **Evaluation only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (5 workloads = 1 eval).\n85\t- **No direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi/evaluator/alternate correctness harness.** This means I\n86\t  **cannot run the kernel locally** to sanity-check numerics; correctness feedback comes *only*\n87\t  from the sanctioned feedback evaluation. ⇒ Design conservatively; spend evals deliberately.\n88\t- Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft/hard 1.0M/1.2M. Ranking = geomean speedup; **every\n89\t  selected workload must pass correctness** (a fast-but-wrong candidate is worthless).\n90\t- Final 16-workload eval is operator-approved only; never auto-run `final`.\n91\t\n92\t### 2.1 Skill applicability (documented, per isolation rule)\n93\t- `KernelWiki`: scoped to Blackwell SM100 / Hopper SM90 (tcgen05, TMEM, TMA, wgmma, 2-SM, NVFP4…).\n94\t  Target is **Ampere sm_80** — none of those primitives exist. **Not applicable**; not invoked.\n95\t- `ncu-report-skill`: profiles B200/sm_100 and requires running Nsight Compute, which is prohibited\n96\t  here and targets the wrong arch. **Not applicable**; not invoked.\n97\t- Conclusion: rely on first-principles Ampere Triton design + feedback-eval evidence.\n98\t\n99\t---\n100\t\n101\t## 3. Numerical risk analysis\n102\t\n103\t### 3.1 Tolerance model\n104\tAssume standard `torch.allclose`-style test: `|a-b| ≤ atol + rtol·|b|`, with `rtol = 0.05` and\n105\t`atol ∈ [0.0025, 0.0038]`. Reasoning about output magnitude:\n106\t- After RMSNorm each Q/K entry is `O(1)` (unit RMS × weight). RoPE keeps it `O(1)`.\n107\t- `scores` after `·scaling` are `O(1)`; softmax weights sum to 1.\n108\t- `V` entries inherit projection magnitude (`std ≈ sqrt(4096)` if inputs ~N(0,1); smaller if inputs\n109\t  are pre-scaled). `attn = p@v` is a convex combination of V rows ⇒ same order as V.\n110\t- `out = attn @ Wo^T` sums 12288 terms ⇒ potentially **large magnitude**.\n111\t\n112\t⇒ For large-magnitude outputs the **relative** term `rtol·|b| = 0.05·|b|` dominates and is very\n113\tgenerous versus bf16 rounding (~0.4% relative). For near-zero outputs, `atol ≈ 0.003` governs.\n114\tEither way, a **fp32-accumulating** implementation should pass comfortably. The tolerance is loose\n115\tenough that flash-style attention (fp32 online softmax) will not be a correctness problem.\n116\t\n117\t### 3.2 Specific precision-matching risks (ordered by importance)\n118\t1. **fp32 accumulation everywhere.** All GEMMs and the P·V accumulation must accumulate in fp32\n119\t   (bf16 accumulation would drift, especially the K=12288 output GEMM). Triton `tl.dot` with fp32\n120\t   accumulator matches cuBLAS behavior.\n121\t2. **RMSNorm in fp32.** Compute `mean(x²)` and `rsqrt` in fp32 (reference upcasts). Casting the\n122\t   *result* back to bf16 before RoPE is the reference behavior; storing normalized Q/K as bf16 is\n123\t   naturally what the attention kernel consumes, so this matches by construction.\n124\t3. **RoPE precision.** Reference does RoPE in **bf16**. Doing RoPE in fp32 then rounding to bf16 is\n125\t   *more* accurate and differs only by sub-ULP; expected to pass. Low risk. Keep note in case a\n126\t   workload is marginal — can force bf16-intermediate RoPE to match exactly.\n127\t4. **Softmax path divergence.** Reference materializes `p` in fp32 then **rounds p to bf16** before\n128\t   `p@v`. Flash attention keeps `p` in fp32 through the accumulation ⇒ slightly *more* accurate.\n129\t   The difference is bounded by per-weight bf16 rounding over the reduction; within tolerance. This\n130\t   is the main intentional numeric deviation and is safe given `rtol=0.05`.\n131\t5. **Scaling placement.** Apply `scaling` to scores (or fold into Q). `q·k` sum over 128 in fp32,\n132\t   multiply by `scaling` in fp32 — matches reference `matmul(...)·scaling` up to fp32 assoc. Safe.\n133\t6. **Causal mask edge.** `diagonal=1` ⇒ key `j > i` masked; the diagonal (`j=i`) is **kept**, so no\n134\t   row is fully masked (every query attends to at least itself) ⇒ no NaN from all `-inf` rows.\n135\t   Flash kernel must include `j ≤ i` (not `j < i`).\n136\t7. **RoPE literal semantics.** Because `cos`/`sin` are random 128-wide tensors (not real angles),\n137\t   do **not** assume `cos[:64]==cos[64:]`; index the full 128 width. `rotate_half` uses the *pre-*\n138\t   scaled x for the rotated term (standard: `out = x*cos + rotate_half(x)*sin`).\n139\t8. **Non-pow2 S masking.** Boundary tiles in both projection (M) and attention (S) must mask OOB\n140\t   rows/keys with `-inf` (scores) / `0` (loads) to avoid contaminating softmax or accumulation.\n141\t9. **bf16 weight load, fp32 math.** Load bf16 operands, cast to fp32 (or let `tl.dot` handle) — same\n142\t   as cuBLAS bf16-in/fp32-accum.\n143\t\n144\t### 3.3 Failure modes to guard against\n145\t- NaN/Inf from unmasked OOB keys in the last attention tile.\n146\t- Wrong GQA head mapping (`kv_head = q_head // 12`) → silently wrong but may still be \"close\" on\n147\t  random data; must get the integer mapping exactly right.\n148\t- RoPE half-swap sign error (`-x[64:]` for the low half, `+x[:64]` for the high half).\n149\t- Reshape/transpose stride mistakes when writing `[B,H,S,D]` vs `[B,S,H,D]`.\n150\t\n151\t---\n152\t\n153\t## 4. Triton design space\n154\t\n155\t### 4.1 Kernel decomposition options\n156\t- **A. Fully separate (baseline-first):** four stages —\n157\t  (1) three projection GEMMs (+bias epilogue), (2) a fused RMSNorm+RoPE elementwise kernel writing\n158\t  Q/K directly into `[B,H,S,D]` layout, (3) flash-attention (GQA, causal) writing `[B,S,H·D]`,\n159\t  (4) output GEMM. Simplest to reason about ⇒ best first correctness candidate.\n160\t- **B. Norm+RoPE fused into flash-attention prologue:** attention kernel loads raw projected Q/K\n161\t  tiles and applies norm+RoPE on the fly. Saves one Q/K memory round-trip. More complex (K tile is\n162\t  re-loaded across M-blocks ⇒ recomputes norm/RoPE); usually still a net win because K/V are small.\n163\t- **C. Fused QKV projection:** one GEMM with vertically-concatenated weights `[14336,4096]`. Needs a\n164\t  one-time weight concat (copy) — amortized only if weights were reused across calls (they are not,\n165\t  per-call inputs) ⇒ likely not worth the concat cost. Keep K and V possibly fused (`[2048,4096]`)\n166\t  since they share M-tiling; Q separate.\n167\t- **D. Fuse output-projection reshape:** attention writes `attn` already in `[M,12288]` row-major so\n168\t  the output GEMM reads it directly (no extra transpose kernel).\n169\t\n170\t**Plan:** start with **A** for a correct, evaluable baseline; then layer B/C/D and tiling as\n171\tseparate candidates guided by feedback speedups.\n172\t\n173\t### 4.2 Projection GEMM design (`x[M,4096] @ W[N,4096]^T + bias`)\n174\t- Standard tiled Triton matmul over `K=4096`; `W` is `[N,K]` so we compute `x·W^T` by loading W with\n175\t  a transposed access pattern (K contiguous → good for `tl.dot(x_tile, w_tile.T)` or load W as\n176\t  `[K,N]` view via strides). Bias added in fp32 epilogue, cast to bf16.\n177\t- Autotune space: `BLOCK_M ∈ {32,64,128}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32,64}`,\n178\t  `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`. Ampere sm_80 ⇒ software-pipelined `cp.async`\n179\t  (Triton handles via `num_stages`). Q proj (N=12288) benefits from larger BLOCK_N; K/V (N=1024)\n180\t  from smaller. Consider separate autotune per GEMM shape.\n181\t- M is small (128–2984) ⇒ grid can be M-light; ensure enough N-tiles × programs to fill the A800's\n182\t  SMs. Possibly split-K only if M very small (M=128 case) — evaluate later.\n183\t\n184\t### 4.3 RMSNorm + RoPE kernel\n185\t- One program per (b, s, head) or per (b, s) processing a block of heads; load the 128-wide head\n186\t  vector, compute `var=mean(x²)` fp32 reduction, `x*rsqrt(var+eps)*weight`, cast bf16.\n187\t- Apply RoPE with `cos/sin[b,s,:]` (broadcast across heads): gather `x_rot` via the ±64 half-swap.\n188\t- Write directly to Q/K buffers laid out `[B,H,S,D]` (attention-friendly, contiguous in D then S).\n189\t- V needs no norm/RoPE; either pass through a copy into `[B,H,S,D]` or have attention read V from the\n190\t  projected `[B,S,8,128]` layout directly (preferred: avoid a copy — attention indexes kv head).\n191\t\n192\t### 4.4 Flash-attention kernel (GQA, causal)\n193\t- Grid: `(num_M_blocks, B·num_heads)` or `(B, H, M_blocks)`. Program computes one `[BLOCK_M,128]`\n194\t  output tile for query head `h`, batch `b`.\n195\t- GQA: `kv_head = h // 12`; K/V loaded from that KV head only (no 12× expansion, no extra memory).\n196\t- Inner loop over key blocks `j`; **causal early-exit**: only iterate `key_block ≤ query_block`, and\n197\t  apply triangular mask (`j ≤ i`) on the diagonal block. Online softmax (running max `m`, running\n198\t  sum `l`, rescale accumulator) in fp32. Fold `scaling` into the score before max.\n199\t- `BLOCK_M ∈ {64,128}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {32,64,128}`, `head_dim=128` fits registers, `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`,\n200\t  `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`. Boundary masking for non-pow2 S on both M and N.\n201\t- **KV-load reuse optimization (later):** since 12 query heads share one KV head, a program that\n202\t  handles a *block of query heads* from the same group amortizes K/V loads (batch the Q of several\n203\t  group-mates). Strong candidate for A800 memory savings; adds register pressure.\n204\t- Output written to `[B,S,H,128]`→`[M,12288]` row-major for the output GEMM.\n205\t\n206\t### 4.5 Output-projection GEMM (`attn[M,12288] @ Wo[4096,12288]^T`)\n207\t- Same tiled-matmul template as projections but `K=12288` (large reduction ⇒ fp32 accum essential),\n208\t  `N=4096`, no bias. Autotune separately; large K favors `BLOCK_K=64`, `num_stages≥3`.\n209\t\n210\t### 4.6 Layout / plumbing decisions\n211\t- Keep everything bf16 in DRAM; fp32 only in registers/accumulators.\n212\t- Precompute strides in Python; pass as kernel args. Avoid `.contiguous()` copies unless a layout is\n213\t  strictly required (attention output already row-major `[M,12288]`).\n214\t- One preallocated output `[B,S,4096]` bf16.\n215\t\n216\t---\n217\t\n218\t## 5. Validation strategy\n219\t\n220\tBecause local execution / alternate correctness harnesses are prohibited, validation is\n221\t**feedback-eval-driven** with heavy up-front paper reasoning:\n222\t\n223\t1. **Static correctness review before each candidate:** re-derive shapes, strides, GQA mapping,\n224\t   RoPE sign pattern, causal condition (`j ≤ i`), OOB masking, fp32 accumulation — against §1.2.\n225\t2. **c001 = correctness-first baseline** (decomposition A, conservative single tiling, no autotune\n226\t   fireworks). Goal: **pass all five workloads** and record a baseline geomean speedup. A correct\n227\t   but only-modestly-fast baseline is the anchor; do not chase speed before correctness is proven.\n228\t3. **Interpret feedback per workload**, not just geomean: a single failing workload (likely the\n229\t   non-pow2 S=293/373 masking cases, or the tiny M=128 case) localizes the bug class.\n230\t4. **One change per candidate ID** (immutable). Sequence of hypotheses:\n231\t   - c001: separate kernels, correct math (establish PASS + baseline).\n232\t   - c00x: autotune / tile the projection + output GEMMs (biggest FLOP share).\n233\t   - c00x: flash-attention tile tuning + causal early-exit.\n234\t   - c00x: fuse norm+RoPE into attention prologue (option B); KV-load reuse across the group of 12.\n235\t   - c00x: K/V fused projection (option C) if profiled-by-feedback beneficial.\n236\t   Each step keeps prior correctness invariants; roll back if a change regresses geomean or breaks a\n237\t   workload.\n238\t5. **Numeric-margin guard:** if any workload is *correct but marginal* (near tolerance), prefer the\n239\t   more-precise variant (bf16-intermediate RoPE / match reference softmax rounding) even at small\n240\t   perf cost — a correctness fail zeroes the workload's contribution.\n241\t6. **Budget discipline:** spend evals on hypotheses with clear expected deltas; stop when geomean\n242\t   converges (diminishing returns across ≥2–3 consecutive candidates) and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`.\n243\t   Never run `final` without operator approval.\n244\t\n245\t### 5.1 Definition of done for the search\n246\t- All five feedback workloads PASS correctness on the chosen best candidate.\n247\t- Geomean speedup > 1.0 and converged (no candidate improving it materially).\n248\t- `candidates.jsonl` has one append-only record per eval (parent, source hash, hypothesis,\n249\t  validation, per-workload results, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage).\n250\t\n251\t---\n252\t\n253\t## 6. Open questions / to confirm via first eval\n254\t- Actual magnitude/scale of \"random\" inputs (governs whether atol or rtol dominates) — inferred from\n255\t  the first feedback correctness result.\n256\t- Whether Triton projection GEMMs on A800 land close enough to cuBLAS that the attention-fusion win\n257\t  yields net geomean > 1 (the central performance bet).\n258\t- Whether the tiny M=128 workload needs split-K or a different tiling to avoid SM under-utilization.\n259\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8df798b-a9b9-45b8-9fc1-9cba86dca820","uuid":"6d50ebc2-725e-434d-8b10-92b798eabc37","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:52:44.993Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-092_gqa_attention_with_qk_norm/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L1/092 GQA Attention with QK Norm (GLM-4.5-Air)\n\nTarget GPU: **NVIDIA A800, `sm_80` (Ampere)**. Primary implementation must be **Triton**;\nPyTorch permitted only for tensor metadata / launch plumbing (no Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension\ncompute fallback). Entry point: `solution/solution.py:run(...)` with the exact signature from\n`task/definition.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation analysis\n\n### 1.1 Fixed configuration (from `definition.json`)\n- `hidden_size = 4096`\n- `num_attention_heads = 96`, `head_dim = 128` → `q_out_features = 96*128 = 12288`\n- `num_key_value_heads = 8` → `kv_out_features = 8*128 = 1024`\n- `num_key_value_groups = 96 / 8 = 12` (each KV head is shared by 12 query heads)\n- `head_dim/2 = 64` (RoPE rotate-half split point)\n- `scaling = head_dim**-0.5 = 128**-0.5 ≈ 0.0883883476`\n- Variable axes: `batch_size (B)`, `seq_len (S)`. All tensors `bfloat16` except `rms_norm_eps` (fp32 scalar).\n\n### 1.2 Reference dataflow (must be numerically reproduced)\nGiven `hidden_states [B,S,4096]`:\n\n1. **Projections** (`F.linear`, i.e. `x @ W^T + b`):\n   - `q = hidden @ q_proj_weight^T + q_proj_bias` → `[B,S,12288]`\n   - `k = hidden @ k_proj_weight^T + k_proj_bias` → `[B,S,1024]`\n   - `v = hidden @ v_proj_weight^T + v_proj_bias` → `[B,S,1024]`\n2. **Reshape to heads**: q→`[B,S,96,128]`, k,v→`[B,S,8,128]`.\n3. **QK RMSNorm** over the last axis (`head_dim=128`), computed in **fp32**:\n   `x_f32 = x.float(); var = mean(x_f32^2, -1, keepdim); x = x_f32 * rsqrt(var + eps);\n    out = (weight * x).to(bf16)`. Applied to q (with `q_norm_weight`) and k (with `k_norm_weight`).\n   **Result is cast back to bf16 *before* RoPE.**\n4. **Transpose** to `[B, H, S, D]` (q: H=96, k/v: H=8).\n5. **RoPE** (`cos`,`sin` are `[B,S,128]`, `unsqueeze(1)`→`[B,1,S,128]`):\n   `rotate_half(x) = cat(-x[...,64:], x[...,:64])`;\n   `x = x*cos + rotate_half(x)*sin`. Applied to q and k in **bf16** (inputs are already bf16).\n   NOTE: `cos`/`sin` are arbitrary random bf16 tensors of full width 128 in the test harness — they are\n   **not** guaranteed to be true cos/sin nor half-duplicated; apply the formula literally element-wise.\n6. **GQA expand**: k,v repeated 8→96 heads (12× each).\n7. **Attention**: `scores = (q @ k^T) * scaling` → `[B,96,S,S]`;\n   add causal mask (`triu(diagonal=1) = -inf`, i.e. key index `> query index` masked);\n   `p = softmax(scores, dim=-1, dtype=fp32).to(bf16)`; `attn = p @ v` → `[B,96,S,128]`.\n8. **Merge heads**: transpose→`[B,S,96,128]`→reshape `[B,S,12288]`.\n9. **Output projection** (no bias): `out = attn @ o_proj_weight^T` → `[B,S,4096]`.\n\n### 1.3 Feedback workloads (five fixed; one eval covers all five)\n| uuid short | B | S | M=B·S | atol | rtol |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ccea3f00 | 1 | 128 | 128 | 0.0034 | 0.05 |\n| 1e96cc88 | 4 | 293 | 1172 | 0.0037 | 0.05 |\n| d0ce8c45 | 1 | 1024 | 1024 | 0.0030 | 0.05 |\n| c92bb0a5 | 1 | 512 | 512 | 0.0025 | 0.05 |\n| 65a1864d | 8 | 373 | 2984 | 0.0038 | 0.05 |\n\nObservations:\n- `S ∈ {128, 293, 373, 512, 1024}` — **two are non-power-of-two (293, 373)**, so every kernel\n  that tiles over `S` (projection M-dim, attention seq-dim) must mask boundary tiles.\n- `M = B·S ∈ {128 … 2984}` — GEMM M-dim is modest; the projection GEMMs are \"tall-skinny→wide\"\n  (`N`=12288 for Q, `K`=4096) and the output GEMM is `N`=4096, `K`=12288.\n- Small S means the `O(S²)` attention is cheap **in FLOPs** but the reference pays a large\n  **memory** cost (materialized `[B,96,S,S]` scores + fp32 softmax buffer + 12× KV expansion).\n\n### 1.4 Roofline / where time goes (rough FLOP accounting, per workload, ×2 for MAC)\nFor the largest case B=8,S=373 (M=2984):\n- Q proj: `M·12288·4096·2 ≈ 300 GFLOP`; K proj + V proj: `M·1024·4096·2·2 ≈ 50 GFLOP`.\n- Attention (QK^T + PV): `B·96·S²·128·2·2 ≈ 8·96·373²·128·4 ≈ 55 GFLOP`.\n- Output proj: `M·4096·12288·2 ≈ 300 GFLOP`.\n\n⇒ **GEMMs dominate FLOPs** (~650 GFLOP vs ~55 GFLOP attention). The reference runs GEMMs on\ncuBLAS (hard to beat) but runs attention as a **naive materialized path with a 12× KV blow-up**.\nTherefore the most reliable speedup source is **fusing/streaming attention (flash-style, no S²\nmaterialization, no KV expansion)**, while the projection/output GEMMs must be re-implemented in\nTriton *at least competitively* with cuBLAS so they don't erase the attention win.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constraints (from CLAUDE.md / TASK.md)\n\n- **Triton-only compute.** No `torch.matmul`/`F.linear`/SDPA in the compute path — those count as a\n  Torch computational fallback → invalid. The three projections, attention, and output projection\n  must all be Triton kernels. PyTorch may only allocate/reshape/stride and launch.\n- **A failed Triton kernel is invalid** — no silent Torch fallback allowed.\n- **Immutability**: candidates `c001, c002, …` are append-only; new source ⇒ new ID.\n- **Evaluation only** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (5 workloads = 1 eval).\n- **No direct CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi/evaluator/alternate correctness harness.** This means I\n  **cannot run the kernel locally** to sanity-check numerics; correctness feedback comes *only*\n  from the sanctioned feedback evaluation. ⇒ Design conservatively; spend evals deliberately.\n- Budget: 100 candidate evals; token soft/hard 1.0M/1.2M. Ranking = geomean speedup; **every\n  selected workload must pass correctness** (a fast-but-wrong candidate is worthless).\n- Final 16-workload eval is operator-approved only; never auto-run `final`.\n\n### 2.1 Skill applicability (documented, per isolation rule)\n- `KernelWiki`: scoped to Blackwell SM100 / Hopper SM90 (tcgen05, TMEM, TMA, wgmma, 2-SM, NVFP4…).\n  Target is **Ampere sm_80** — none of those primitives exist. **Not applicable**; not invoked.\n- `ncu-report-skill`: profiles B200/sm_100 and requires running Nsight Compute, which is prohibited\n  here and targets the wrong arch. **Not applicable**; not invoked.\n- Conclusion: rely on first-principles Ampere Triton design + feedback-eval evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Numerical risk analysis\n\n### 3.1 Tolerance model\nAssume standard `torch.allclose`-style test: `|a-b| ≤ atol + rtol·|b|`, with `rtol = 0.05` and\n`atol ∈ [0.0025, 0.0038]`. Reasoning about output magnitude:\n- After RMSNorm each Q/K entry is `O(1)` (unit RMS × weight). RoPE keeps it `O(1)`.\n- `scores` after `·scaling` are `O(1)`; softmax weights sum to 1.\n- `V` entries inherit projection magnitude (`std ≈ sqrt(4096)` if inputs ~N(0,1); smaller if inputs\n  are pre-scaled). `attn = p@v` is a convex combination of V rows ⇒ same order as V.\n- `out = attn @ Wo^T` sums 12288 terms ⇒ potentially **large magnitude**.\n\n⇒ For large-magnitude outputs the **relative** term `rtol·|b| = 0.05·|b|` dominates and is very\ngenerous versus bf16 rounding (~0.4% relative). For near-zero outputs, `atol ≈ 0.003` governs.\nEither way, a **fp32-accumulating** implementation should pass comfortably. The tolerance is loose\nenough that flash-style attention (fp32 online softmax) will not be a correctness problem.\n\n### 3.2 Specific precision-matching risks (ordered by importance)\n1. **fp32 accumulation everywhere.** All GEMMs and the P·V accumulation must accumulate in fp32\n   (bf16 accumulation would drift, especially the K=12288 output GEMM). Triton `tl.dot` with fp32\n   accumulator matches cuBLAS behavior.\n2. **RMSNorm in fp32.** Compute `mean(x²)` and `rsqrt` in fp32 (reference upcasts). Casting the\n   *result* back to bf16 before RoPE is the reference behavior; storing normalized Q/K as bf16 is\n   naturally what the attention kernel consumes, so this matches by construction.\n3. **RoPE precision.** Reference does RoPE in **bf16**. Doing RoPE in fp32 then rounding to bf16 is\n   *more* accurate and differs only by sub-ULP; expected to pass. Low risk. Keep note in case a\n   workload is marginal — can force bf16-intermediate RoPE to match exactly.\n4. **Softmax path divergence.** Reference materializes `p` in fp32 then **rounds p to bf16** before\n   `p@v`. Flash attention keeps `p` in fp32 through the accumulation ⇒ slightly *more* accurate.\n   The difference is bounded by per-weight bf16 rounding over the reduction; within tolerance. This\n   is the main intentional numeric deviation and is safe given `rtol=0.05`.\n5. **Scaling placement.** Apply `scaling` to scores (or fold into Q). `q·k` sum over 128 in fp32,\n   multiply by `scaling` in fp32 — matches reference `matmul(...)·scaling` up to fp32 assoc. Safe.\n6. **Causal mask edge.** `diagonal=1` ⇒ key `j > i` masked; the diagonal (`j=i`) is **kept**, so no\n   row is fully masked (every query attends to at least itself) ⇒ no NaN from all `-inf` rows.\n   Flash kernel must include `j ≤ i` (not `j < i`).\n7. **RoPE literal semantics.** Because `cos`/`sin` are random 128-wide tensors (not real angles),\n   do **not** assume `cos[:64]==cos[64:]`; index the full 128 width. `rotate_half` uses the *pre-*\n   scaled x for the rotated term (standard: `out = x*cos + rotate_half(x)*sin`).\n8. **Non-pow2 S masking.** Boundary tiles in both projection (M) and attention (S) must mask OOB\n   rows/keys with `-inf` (scores) / `0` (loads) to avoid contaminating softmax or accumulation.\n9. **bf16 weight load, fp32 math.** Load bf16 operands, cast to fp32 (or let `tl.dot` handle) — same\n   as cuBLAS bf16-in/fp32-accum.\n\n### 3.3 Failure modes to guard against\n- NaN/Inf from unmasked OOB keys in the last attention tile.\n- Wrong GQA head mapping (`kv_head = q_head // 12`) → silently wrong but may still be \"close\" on\n  random data; must get the integer mapping exactly right.\n- RoPE half-swap sign error (`-x[64:]` for the low half, `+x[:64]` for the high half).\n- Reshape/transpose stride mistakes when writing `[B,H,S,D]` vs `[B,S,H,D]`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Triton design space\n\n### 4.1 Kernel decomposition options\n- **A. Fully separate (baseline-first):** four stages —\n  (1) three projection GEMMs (+bias epilogue), (2) a fused RMSNorm+RoPE elementwise kernel writing\n  Q/K directly into `[B,H,S,D]` layout, (3) flash-attention (GQA, causal) writing `[B,S,H·D]`,\n  (4) output GEMM. Simplest to reason about ⇒ best first correctness candidate.\n- **B. Norm+RoPE fused into flash-attention prologue:** attention kernel loads raw projected Q/K\n  tiles and applies norm+RoPE on the fly. Saves one Q/K memory round-trip. More complex (K tile is\n  re-loaded across M-blocks ⇒ recomputes norm/RoPE); usually still a net win because K/V are small.\n- **C. Fused QKV projection:** one GEMM with vertically-concatenated weights `[14336,4096]`. Needs a\n  one-time weight concat (copy) — amortized only if weights were reused across calls (they are not,\n  per-call inputs) ⇒ likely not worth the concat cost. Keep K and V possibly fused (`[2048,4096]`)\n  since they share M-tiling; Q separate.\n- **D. Fuse output-projection reshape:** attention writes `attn` already in `[M,12288]` row-major so\n  the output GEMM reads it directly (no extra transpose kernel).\n\n**Plan:** start with **A** for a correct, evaluable baseline; then layer B/C/D and tiling as\nseparate candidates guided by feedback speedups.\n\n### 4.2 Projection GEMM design (`x[M,4096] @ W[N,4096]^T + bias`)\n- Standard tiled Triton matmul over `K=4096`; `W` is `[N,K]` so we compute `x·W^T` by loading W with\n  a transposed access pattern (K contiguous → good for `tl.dot(x_tile, w_tile.T)` or load W as\n  `[K,N]` view via strides). Bias added in fp32 epilogue, cast to bf16.\n- Autotune space: `BLOCK_M ∈ {32,64,128}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {64,128,256}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32,64}`,\n  `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`. Ampere sm_80 ⇒ software-pipelined `cp.async`\n  (Triton handles via `num_stages`). Q proj (N=12288) benefits from larger BLOCK_N; K/V (N=1024)\n  from smaller. Consider separate autotune per GEMM shape.\n- M is small (128–2984) ⇒ grid can be M-light; ensure enough N-tiles × programs to fill the A800's\n  SMs. Possibly split-K only if M very small (M=128 case) — evaluate later.\n\n### 4.3 RMSNorm + RoPE kernel\n- One program per (b, s, head) or per (b, s) processing a block of heads; load the 128-wide head\n  vector, compute `var=mean(x²)` fp32 reduction, `x*rsqrt(var+eps)*weight`, cast bf16.\n- Apply RoPE with `cos/sin[b,s,:]` (broadcast across heads): gather `x_rot` via the ±64 half-swap.\n- Write directly to Q/K buffers laid out `[B,H,S,D]` (attention-friendly, contiguous in D then S).\n- V needs no norm/RoPE; either pass through a copy into `[B,H,S,D]` or have attention read V from the\n  projected `[B,S,8,128]` layout directly (preferred: avoid a copy — attention indexes kv head).\n\n### 4.4 Flash-attention kernel (GQA, causal)\n- Grid: `(num_M_blocks, B·num_heads)` or `(B, H, M_blocks)`. Program computes one `[BLOCK_M,128]`\n  output tile for query head `h`, batch `b`.\n- GQA: `kv_head = h // 12`; K/V loaded from that KV head only (no 12× expansion, no extra memory).\n- Inner loop over key blocks `j`; **causal early-exit**: only iterate `key_block ≤ query_block`, and\n  apply triangular mask (`j ≤ i`) on the diagonal block. Online softmax (running max `m`, running\n  sum `l`, rescale accumulator) in fp32. Fold `scaling` into the score before max.\n- `BLOCK_M ∈ {64,128}`, `BLOCK_N ∈ {32,64,128}`, `head_dim=128` fits registers, `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`,\n  `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`. Boundary masking for non-pow2 S on both M and N.\n- **KV-load reuse optimization (later):** since 12 query heads share one KV head, a program that\n  handles a *block of query heads* from the same group amortizes K/V loads (batch the Q of several\n  group-mates). Strong candidate for A800 memory savings; adds register pressure.\n- Output written to `[B,S,H,128]`→`[M,12288]` row-major for the output GEMM.\n\n### 4.5 Output-projection GEMM (`attn[M,12288] @ Wo[4096,12288]^T`)\n- Same tiled-matmul template as projections but `K=12288` (large reduction ⇒ fp32 accum essential),\n  `N=4096`, no bias. Autotune separately; large K favors `BLOCK_K=64`, `num_stages≥3`.\n\n### 4.6 Layout / plumbing decisions\n- Keep everything bf16 in DRAM; fp32 only in registers/accumulators.\n- Precompute strides in Python; pass as kernel args. Avoid `.contiguous()` copies unless a layout is\n  strictly required (attention output already row-major `[M,12288]`).\n- One preallocated output `[B,S,4096]` bf16.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Validation strategy\n\nBecause local execution / alternate correctness harnesses are prohibited, validation is\n**feedback-eval-driven** with heavy up-front paper reasoning:\n\n1. **Static correctness review before each candidate:** re-derive shapes, strides, GQA mapping,\n   RoPE sign pattern, causal condition (`j ≤ i`), OOB masking, fp32 accumulation — against §1.2.\n2. **c001 = correctness-first baseline** (decomposition A, conservative single tiling, no autotune\n   fireworks). Goal: **pass all five workloads** and record a baseline geomean speedup. A correct\n   but only-modestly-fast baseline is the anchor; do not chase speed before correctness is proven.\n3. **Interpret feedback per workload**, not just geomean: a single failing workload (likely the\n   non-pow2 S=293/373 masking cases, or the tiny M=128 case) localizes the bug class.\n4. **One change per candidate ID** (immutable). Sequence of hypotheses:\n   - c001: separate kernels, correct math (establish PASS + baseline).\n   - c00x: autotune / tile the projection + output GEMMs (biggest FLOP share).\n   - c00x: flash-attention tile tuning + causal early-exit.\n   - c00x: fuse norm+RoPE into attention prologue (option B); KV-load reuse across the group of 12.\n   - c00x: K/V fused projection (option C) if profiled-by-feedback beneficial.\n   Each step keeps prior correctness invariants; roll back if a change regresses geomean or breaks a\n   workload.\n5. **Numeric-margin guard:** if any workload is *correct but marginal* (near tolerance), prefer the\n   more-precise variant (bf16-intermediate RoPE / match reference softmax rounding) even at small\n   perf cost — a correctness fail zeroes the workload's contribution.\n6. **Budget discipline:** spend evals on hypotheses with clear expected deltas; stop when geomean\n   converges (diminishing returns across ≥2–3 consecutive candidates) and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`.\n   Never run `final` without operator approval.\n\n### 5.1 Definition of done for the search\n- All five feedback workloads PASS correctness on the chosen best candidate.\n- Geomean speedup > 1.0 and converged (no candidate improving it materially).\n- `candidates.jsonl` has one append-only record per eval (parent, source hash, hypothesis,\n  validation, per-workload results, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Open questions / to confirm via first eval\n- Actual magnitude/scale of \"random\" inputs (governs whether atol or rtol dominates) — inferred from\n  the first feedback correctness result.\n- Whether Triton projection GEMMs on A800 land close enough to cuBLAS that the attention-fusion win\n  yields net geomean > 1 (the central performance bet).\n- Whether the tiny M=128 workload needs split-K or a different tiling to avoid SM under-utilization.\n","numLines":259,"startLine":1,"totalLines":259}}}
